SOLUTION
To automate the collection of Apache logs from App Server 3 (stapp03) and store them on the Storage Server (ststor01), you need to create a Jenkins Freestyle job that runs a shell script on a schedule. The script will securely copy the logs using scp after establishing passwordless SSH access from the Jenkins server to both target servers.

Below is a step-by-step guide based on verified solutions for this KodeKloud task.

🔑 Step 1: Configure Passwordless SSH from the Jenkins Server
Jenkins must be able to connect to stapp03 (as user tony) and ststor01 (as user natasha) without being prompted for a password.

Open the Lab Terminal (usually connected to the jump-host) and SSH into the Jenkins server:

bash
ssh jenkins@jenkins
# Password: j@rv!s
Generate an SSH key pair if one does not already exist:

bash
ssh-keygen -t rsa -N "" -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa
Copy the public key to App Server 3 (stapp03) for user tony:

bash
ssh-copy-id tony@stapp03
# Password: Ir0nM@n
Copy the public key to the Storage Server (ststor01) for user natasha:

bash
ssh-copy-id natasha@ststor01
# Password: Bl@kW
Verify that passwordless login works for both servers:

bash
ssh tony@stapp03 hostname   # Should output: stapp03
ssh natasha@ststor01 hostname # Should output: ststor01
These steps are critical to allow Jenkins to run scp commands without manual intervention.

🛠️ Step 2: Log In to Jenkins and Install Necessary Plugins
Click the Jenkins button on the top bar and log in with:

Username: admin

Password: Adm!n321

Navigate to Manage Jenkins → Manage Plugins.

In the Available tab, install the following plugins (they are useful for SSH-based operations, though the shell script below can also work with just the built-in SSH client):

SSH

SSH Credentials

Publish Over SSH (optional, but often used for remote transfers)

After installation, restart Jenkins when prompted (check the “Restart Jenkins when installation is complete” option). Refresh the UI if it gets stuck.

📝 Step 3: Create the Freestyle Job
From the Jenkins dashboard, click New Item.

Enter the name copy-logs.

Select Freestyle project and click OK.

⏰ Step 4: Configure the Build Trigger (Cron Schedule)
In the job configuration, scroll to the Build Triggers section.

Check the box Build periodically.

In the Schedule field, enter the exact expression:

text
*/10 * * * *
⚠️ Important: Do not use H/10 * * * * or any other variation. The lab validator checks for this exact string.

🖥️ Step 5: Add the Build Step (Shell Script)
Scroll to the Build section.

Click Add build step and choose Execute shell.

Paste the following script. This script uses scp to first pull the logs from stapp03 to a temporary directory on the Jenkins server, and then pushes them to ststor01 under /usr/src/dba:

bash
mkdir -p /tmp/apache-logs
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp03:/var/log/httpd/access_log /tmp/apache-logs/
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp03:/var/log/httpd/error_log /tmp/apache-logs/
scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no /tmp/apache-logs/* natasha@ststor01:/usr/src/dba/
rm -rf /tmp/apache-logs
Explanation of the script:

mkdir -p /tmp/apache-logs creates a temporary working directory on the Jenkins server.

The two scp commands pull the access_log and error_log files from stapp03 into the temporary directory.

The third scp command pushes all files from the temporary directory to /usr/src/dba/ on ststor01 using the natasha user.

rm -rf /tmp/apache-logs cleans up the temporary directory.

Alternative: If you prefer to use the Publish Over SSH plugin, you can configure the SSH server details and use its “Send files or execute commands over SSH” build step. However, the shell script above is simpler and directly fulfills the requirement.

💾 Step 6: Save, Build, and Verify
Click Save to apply the job configuration.

Click Build Now to trigger a manual run and verify the job works immediately.

Check the Console Output of the build to ensure the scp commands executed without errors.

Log in to the Storage Server (ststor01) as natasha and verify the log files exist in the destination directory:

bash
ssh natasha@ststor01
ls -l /usr/src/dba/
You should see access_log and error_log in that directory.

